=== task: code medium
title: A Countdown on One Line
patterns: end + lines
prompt: Print this countdown with four prints, one for each of `3`, `2`, `1` and `Go!`, all on one line:
```output
3 2 1 Go!
```
**Constraints:** use four prints and `end=`.
tests: stdout
starter:
```python
# one print for each part of the countdown
```
solution:
```python
print(3, end=" ")
print(2, end=" ")
print(1, end=" ")
print("Go!")
```
require[end=]: Keep the countdown on one line with `end=`.
forbid[3 2]: Use a separate print for each part.
hint: The first three prints must end with a space instead of a line break.
hint: `print(3, end=" ")`, and the same for 2 and 1. The last print ends normally.
right: Each `end=" "` swaps a line break for a space, so the next print continues the line. The last print keeps its line break, which finishes the line.
wrong: Write `print(3, end=" ")`, `print(2, end=" ")`, `print(1, end=" ")` and then `print("Go!")`.

